If $f(a)=b$ and $f(b)=4$, then what is the value of $a-b$?

answer:
Step 1: A rotation about the origin in $\mathbb{R}^2$ by an angle $\theta$ counterclockwise is represented by the matrix:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Step 2: A clockwise rotation by $45^\circ$ is equivalent to a counterclockwise rotation by $-45^\circ$. So set $\theta = -45^\circ$.

Step 3: Compute $\cos(-45^\circ)$ and $\sin(-45^\circ)$:
$$
\cos(-45^\circ) = \cos(45^\circ) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \quad \sin(-45^\circ) = -\sin(45^\circ) = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
$$

Step 4: Substitute into the rotation matrix:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos(-45^\circ) & -\sin(-45^\circ) \\
\sin(-45^\circ) & \cos(-45^\circ)
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & -(-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\
-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{bmatrix}
$$

\boxed{\begin{bmatrix} \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\ -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \end{bmatrix}}

